WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
Ownership and lifetime rules are central to secure C++ API design.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:12.760
An interface should make it clear who owns an object, who may borrow it, who may store access, and who is responsible for cleanup.

3
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.360
RAII is the preferred foundation.

4
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:24.100
Resource-owning objects should clean up through destructors, and APIs should avoid hidden ownership transfer that callers cannot see from the type or name.

5
00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.780
std::unique_ptr communicates exclusive ownership transfer.

6
00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:36.080
std::shared_ptr should indicate intentional shared lifetime, not uncertainty about who owns the object.

7
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:41.380
std::weak_ptr is useful for observation when shared ownership already exists.

8
00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.700
References are good for short-lived required borrows.

9
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.320
Raw pointers can represent optional non-owning access when the convention is clear.

10
00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:58.340
Whatever pattern is chosen, the API should not leave callers guessing whether they must delete, retain, or avoid storing the value.

11
00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:06.140
View types such as std::span can safely express bounded non-owning data only when the source lifetime is clear.

12
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.960
An API that stores a view must document and enforce how long the source remains valid.

13
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:13.240
Callbacks need lifetime contracts.

14
00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:25.420
If a callback may run later, on another thread, or after the registering object changes state, the API must define what may be captured, when callbacks stop, and how unregistration works.

15
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.660
Resource boundaries should be boring and obvious.

16
00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:38.520
A secure API makes cleanup responsibility visible, avoids surprise retention, and preserves valid lifetime relationships across normal and failure paths.

17
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.280
Handle types can help when resources are managed by a subsystem.

18
00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:50.020
A handle should have clear validity rules, close behavior, move behavior, and thread-safety expectations.

19
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:54.140
Avoid APIs that accept a pointer and later store it without saying so.

20
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.240
That pattern turns a short borrow into an implicit lifetime requirement.

21
00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:07.720
Reviewers should trace every object crossing the boundary and ask whether its owner, borrower, cleanup path, and storage policy are visible.
